 employees that Unisys <ei28 said> a month ago would be <ei30 laid> off . Unisys <ei31 said> that should <ei32 help> it <ei33 save> $ 500 million a year in costs , again at the high end of the previously reported range of $ 400 million to $ 500 million . The company also <ei34 took> a <ei35 write-off> of $ 150 million to <ei36 cover> <ei37 losses> on some fixed-price defense contracts , as some new managers <ei38 took> a hard <ei39 look> at the prospects for that slow-growing business . In addition , Unisys <ei40 set> up an unspecified reserve -- apparently $ 60 million to $ 70 million -- to <ei41 cover> the minimum amount it will have to <ei42 pay> the government because of its <ei44 involvement> in the defense-procurement <ei45 scandal> . Unisys also <ei46 noted> that it <ei47 paid> $ 78.8 million in taxes during the quarter , even though tax payments normally would be minimal in a quarter that produced such a big loss . The tax payments will <ei49 leave> Unisys with $ 225 million in loss carry-forwards that will <ei50 cut> tax <ei51 payments> in future quarters . In addition , Unisys <ei53 said> it <ei54 reduced> computer inventories a further $ 100 million during the quarter , <ei56 leaving> it within $ 100 million of its goal of a <ei57 reduction> of $ 500 million by the end of the year . Still , Unisys <ei59 said> its European business was <ei60 weak> during the quarter , a worrisome sign given that the company has <ei62 relied> on solid results overseas to <ei63 overcome> weakness in the U.S. over the past several quarters . The company also <ei65 reported> slower <ei66 growth> in another important business : systems that use the Unix operating system . That would be a huge <ei67 problem> if it were to <ei68 continue> , because Unisys is <ei70 betting> its business on the assumption that customers <ei71 want> to <ei72 move> away from using operating systems that run on only one manufacturer 's equipment and toward systems -- mainly Unix -- that work on almost anyone 's machines . In addition , Unisys must <ei73 deal> with its increasingly oppressive debt load . Debt has <ei74 risen> to around $ 4 billion , or about 50 % of total capitalization . That means Unisys must <ei75 pay> about $ 100 million in interest every quarter , on top of $ 27 million in dividends on preferred stock . Jim Unruh , Unisys 's president , <ei77 said> he is <ei78 approaching> next year with caution . He <ei79 said> the strength of the world-wide economy is <ei80 suspect> , and does n't see much revenue growth in the cards . He also <ei81 said> that the price wars <ei82 flaring> up in parts of the computer industry will <ei83 continue> through next year . He <ei85 said> the <ei86 move> toward standard operating systems means customers are n't <ei87 locked> into buying from their traditional computer supplier and can force prices down . That , he <ei89 said> , is why Unisys is <ei90 overhauling> its whole business : It <ei91 needs> to <ei92 prepare> for a world in which profit margins will be <ei93 lower> than computer companies have been <ei94 used> to . \" We 've <ei95 approached> this not as a response to a temporary condition in the industry but as a fundamental change the industry is going through , \" Mr. Unruh <ei96 said> . \" The information-systems industry is still going to be a high-growth <ei97 business> , and we 're <ei98 confident> that we <ei99 have> tremendous assets as a company . But we do n't <ei100 minimize> the <ei101 challenges> of the near term . \" Securities analysts were even more <ei103 cautious> , having been <ei104 burned> repeatedly on Unisys this year . Some had <ei106 predicted> <ei107 earnings> of more than $ 4 a share for this year , up from last year 's fully diluted $ 3.27 a share on earnings of $ 680.6 million . But the company <ei110 said> Friday that it <ei112 had> <ei113 losses> of $ 673.3 million through the first nine months , compared with <ei115 earnings> a year earlier of $ 382.2 million , or $ 2.22 a 